          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What kind of activity is described for someone with COPD who gets breathless walking?",
          "answer": "Activity is described as a core treatment for COPD. The main source I found says breathlessness on exertion often leads people to avoid activity, which weakens the muscles and makes breathlessness come sooner. Breaking that cycle is a central goal. The retrieved passages do not describe pacing, graded walking, or a specific walking routine in detail. The activity note I found covers only this background. (Source: *Staying active with COPD*, lifestyle summary.)\n\nThe COPD guideline summary says pulmonary rehabilitation improves breathlessness and exercise capacity more than any inhaler does. It is a structured programme that combines supervised exercise with education. (Source: *COPD: spirometric diagnosis, group assessment and initial therapy*, guideline summary.) It is the main programme named in the material I searched, so it is a sensible topic to raise with a clinician or respiratory team.\n\n**Seek urgent medical help, not a routine appointment, if breathlessness:**\n- Is getting worse and does not respond to the usual reliever inhaler.\n- Happens at rest.\n- Comes with confusion or unusual drowsiness. In someone struggling to breathe, drowsiness can be a serious sign.\n- Comes with blue lips or fingers, chest pain, coughing up blood, or fever with a marked change in phlegm.\n\n(Source: *Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)*, condition summary.)\n\nIf you are unsure whether your situation is urgent, contact a doctor or your local emergency service. For a specific walking plan or pulmonary rehabilitation referral, ask a clinician, who can assess your breathing and tailor advice to you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
